%I A400365 #18 Oct 01 2026 22:35:51
%S A400365 1685,277693,930257,10279949,56290357,105229001,249909893,791453101,
%T A400365 1055509993,3851336249,4338274657,9718179701,8887310749,17786489393,
%U A400365 24868262057,58247854949,55860034957,129492617693,190030148101,169462646593,284963510357,549234351349
%N A400365 Center parameters of a six-square 3 X 3 magic-square construction from primitive Pythagorean triangles with prime hypotenuse.
%C A400365 For p = A002144(n), let q = A400364(n) and Q = a(n) = p*q.
%C A400365 There are unique integers x > y > 0 such that p = x^2 + y^2.
%C A400365 Let u = x^2 - y^2, v = 2*x*y, C = Q^2, X + i*Y = (u + i*v)*(u^2 - i*v^2)^2, d = 2*u*v*q^2, e = 2*X*Y, R = (u+v)*(q - 4*u*v*(u^2 - u*v + v^2)).
%C A400365 Then the array with rows
%C A400365   [C+e,     C+d, C-d-e],
%C A400365   [C-d-2*e, C,   C+d+2*e],
%C A400365   [C+d+e,   C-d, C-e]
%C A400365 is a 3 X 3 magic square with magic sum 3*C. The six entries C+e, C+d, C-d-2*e, C, C-d and C-e are respectively (X+Y)^2, (q*(u+v))^2, R^2, Q^2, (q*(u-v))^2 and (X-Y)^2.
%C A400365 All nine entries are positive and distinct; see the linked note for the proof. The construction works whether q is prime or composite.
%C A400365 The terms are listed in increasing order of p, not in increasing order of Q.
%C A400365 Since gcd(p,q) = 1, Q is a semiprime exactly when the corresponding p belongs to A400363.
%C A400365 For background on 3 X 3 magic squares with six square entries, see Morgenstern's discussion of Euler's formulas and Boyer's work linked below.
%H A400365 Edward Schmidt, <a href="/A400365/b400365.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..2549</a>
%H A400365 Christian Boyer, <a href="https://www.multimagie.com/English/SquaresOfSquares.htm">Magic squares of squares</a>.
%H A400365 Christian Boyer, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1007/BF02985794">Some notes on the magic squares of squares problem</a>, The Mathematical Intelligencer, 27 (2005), 52-64.
%H A400365 Christian Boyer, <a href="https://www.multimagie.com/English/SquaresOfSquaresSearch.htm">Latest research on the "3x3 magic square of squares" problem</a>.
%H A400365 Lee Morgenstern, <a href="https://www.multimagie.com/English/Morgenstern14.htm">Euler's Formulas for 3x3 Magic Squares of 6 Squares</a>, December 2011.
%H A400365 Edward Schmidt, <a href="/A400365/a400365.txt">Fourth powers of Pythagorean legs and a magic square with six squares</a>.
%F A400365 a(n) = A002144(n)*A400364(n).
%e A400365 For p = 5, q = 3^4 + 4^4 = 337 and a(1) = 5*337 = 1685. The construction gives
%e A400365   [    937^2, 2359^2, 2074825 ]
%e A400365   [   2009^2, 1685^2, 1642369 ]
%e A400365   [  3603625,  337^2,  2191^2 ]
%e A400365 Every row, column and diagonal sums to 3*1685^2 = 8517675. All nine entries are positive and distinct, and exactly six of them are squares.
%e A400365 For p = 13, q = 5^4 + 12^4 = 21361 is composite and a(2) = 13*21361 = 277693; the same construction again gives a magic square with six square entries.
%o A400365 (Python)
%o A400365 from math import isqrt
%o A400365 from sympy import primerange
%o A400365 def a400365(limit=50000):
%o A400365     # Return terms for hypotenuses p < limit.
%o A400365     out = []
%o A400365     for p in primerange(5, limit):
%o A400365         if p % 4 != 1:
%o A400365             continue
%o A400365         for y in range(1, isqrt(p // 2) + 1):
%o A400365             x = isqrt(p - y*y)
%o A400365             if x*x + y*y == p:
%o A400365                 out.append(p*((x*x - y*y)**4 + (2*x*y)**4))
%o A400365                 break
%o A400365     return out
%Y A400365 Cf. A002144, A002365, A002366, A400363, A400364.
%K A400365 nonn,easy,new
%O A400365 1,1
%A A400365 _Edward Schmidt_, Sep 24 2026
